{
  "id": "azure-app-registration",
  "provider": "azure",
  "basics": true,
  "order": 3,
  "title": "App registration and service principal",
  "summary": "An app registration is the global definition of an application in its home tenant. A service principal (an 'enterprise application') is that app's identity in each tenant that uses it, and it's the service principal that receives permissions.",
  "notes": [
    "One app registration, many service principals: a multi-tenant app gets a service principal in every tenant that consents to it.",
    "Credentials belong to the app registration. Prefer federated credentials or certificates over client secrets, which are long-lived and easy to leak.",
    "Two kinds of permission land on the service principal: API permissions (e.g. Microsoft Graph, delegated or application, often needing admin consent) and Azure RBAC role assignments on resources.",
    "Owners of an app registration can add credentials to it, and so can sign in as it. Treat ownership of a privileged app as privileged access.",
    "Code running in Azure usually doesn't need an app registration at all: a managed identity is a service principal without any credential to manage."
  ],
